00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:37.490
to draw a straight--

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:41.960
No, flat plane in the 3-D
space like a flat plane

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:48.660
here to classify the red
part and the blue part.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:50.940
There's another
image and that tells

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:55.130
about how the hidden layer
can tweak the data to extract

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:57.000
the future set.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:00.195
You see, even if you have a very
complex structure inside data

00:15:00.195 --> 00:15:02.750
set, the hidden layer
can try to tweak

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:08.110
the data in every dimension
to make it classifiable.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:14.560
So by using these
technologies, you

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:18.330
can even have this double
spiral problem recognized

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:19.580
by the neural networks.

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:24.160
Let's take a look at the
actual demonstration for this.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:28.640
You go to the third
demonstrations.

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:31.676
Here we have much complex--

00:15:31.676 --> 00:15:33.175
Now we have the
three hidden layers.

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:38.300
And the data set is
much more complex.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:40.600
It's a called double spiral.

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:43.910
So as long as you are using
the traditional IT programming

00:15:43.910 --> 00:15:47.590
language, it's really harder
to solve this problem.

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:50.690
If you have to solve this
problem by using the Java

00:15:50.690 --> 00:15:54.080
or Python with the ordinary
programming paradigm,

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:57.020
do you want to write many
statements with many thresholds

00:15:57.020 --> 00:16:00.240
to check whether each data
point is in this area?

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:01.730
Or this area?

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:04.420
Maybe you can do that by writing
ten [INAUDIBLE] statements,

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:06.290
but I wouldn't do that.

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:10.010
Instead, I want to use neural
networks to solve this problem.

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:14.880
Because by taking
some time, especially

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:18.270
computing power and
training data set,

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:21.390
the computer can gradually
extract the hidden patterns

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:23.940
inside the data set.

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:28.000
Just like you're
watching right now.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:31.300
So initially, the neural
networks cannot understand

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:33.910
what's going on
inside the data set.

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:38.370
But if you keep teaching
the neural networks,

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:42.100
than the neural network tried
to tweak all the weights

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:43.980
and biases.

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:45.930
Increasing or
decreasing each weights

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:48.600
and biases a little
bit, and see which

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:52.710
direction would be the best way
to converge all the weights--

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:54.510
combination of
weights and biases.

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:59.660
And now, you see the neural
network training is converging.

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:02.730
And now you get neural
networks smart enough

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:04.302
to solve this problem.

00:17:04.302 --> 00:17:06.510
And again, if you take a
look at what each of neurons

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.190
are doing in your
neural networks,

00:17:08.190 --> 00:17:09.869
the neurons in the
[INAUDIBLE] layer

00:17:09.869 --> 00:17:11.970
are doing very simple things.

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:14.400
Draw a straight line and
classify the data points

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:17.180
in two areas.

00:17:17.180 --> 00:17:19.562
The neurons in the
second layer are doing

00:17:19.562 --> 00:17:20.770
a really, really smart thing.

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:26.869
These neurons can
recognize a little bit

00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:29.540
more complex pattern.

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:34.030
And in the third layer, you
can see much smarter neurons.

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:36.970
And output layer, you
could have a single failure

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:40.300
that is smart enough to
classify it as a spiral

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:42.620
just like humans do.

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:45.230
So this is how the
neural network works.

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:48.520
And this is the reason why
the deep neural networks

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:51.920
can sometimes exceed
human performance.

00:17:51.920 --> 00:17:54.860
Because it's not
instructed by humans.

00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:58.100
As long as you keep instructing
computers how to solve

00:17:58.100 --> 00:18:01.940
a particular problem, it cannot
exceed human performance.

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:04.730
But if you let the computers
try to find the best

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:06.990
way to solve a
particular problem,

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:09.170
sometimes it's
possible that computers

00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:12.230
can find the best way
to extract the feature

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:13.220
to solve its problems.

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:20.040
So that was my part.

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:23.690
So I'd like to pass this Lak.

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:25.030
LAK LAKSHMANAN: Thank you Kaz.

00:18:25.030 --> 00:18:27.480
So my name is Lak.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:29.640
I'm part of the
professional services

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:31.241
organization at Google.

00:18:31.241 --> 00:18:33.240
And I've been doing machine
learning pretty much

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:34.080
all my career.

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:39.780
So whenever I see this slide,
it always sets me back.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:42.540
Because the very first thing
that I did out of college--

00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:45.780
I used to work at NOAA, the
US Weather Research Agency.

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:48.840
And building models,
and I started out

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:50.670
doing image-processing.

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:54.960
So when Kaz said, people
would write filters,

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:56.100
that people was me.

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:56.610
Right?

00:18:56.610 --> 00:18:59.760
So out of college, I was
writing image-processing filters

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:02.490
to find thunderstorms
and radar images.

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:05.250
And we'd basically develop
all of these image-processing

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:06.540
filters by hand.

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:10.290
My PhD was in
texture processing.

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:13.860
And so when you then
go ahead, and you

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:15.900
look at a deep neural net--

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:18.390
And you basically say, when
you look at a deep neural net,

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:21.150
the first part of a neural
net are about basically

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:22.800
finding edges.

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:24.960
You're basically finding
very simple shapes,

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:27.270
and the next part
of the neural net

00:19:27.270 --> 00:19:30.030
is about finding more
complex shapes, and so on.

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:33.870
My work was, essentially,
probably around the third layer

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:35.580
of the neural network.

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:39.660
But if you go look at modern
day image classification neural

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:44.230
network, the number of
layers ranges to about 130,

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:46.020
140 layers.

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:49.770
So you can imagine what
goes through my mind

00:19:49.770 --> 00:19:52.890
every time I look at
modern neural network.

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:55.440
And to basically think, we were
working on these such things

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:57.210
very primitively.

00:19:57.210 --> 00:19:59.640
The second part of the
story is, of course,

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:02.610
neural networks have been
around since the 1970s.

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:04.930
So we could have used neural
networks, where I did.

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:05.430
And I did.

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:06.900
I basically did.

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:09.330
We did a whole bunch of
things with neural networks.

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:11.700
And I remember a
conversation, many years ago,

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:14.700
where we said, hey instead of
us was doing all these filters

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:19.020
by hand, why did they just throw
the raw pixels out of our radar

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:22.830
images into a neural network
and have it figure it out?

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:27.120
And then we all had a big laugh,
because how could you ever get

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:32.610
the computational power required
to throw your raw pixels

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:34.140
into your neural network?

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:37.590
Can you imagine the number of
layers that it's going to need?

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:41.160
How on earth are you going
to program a neural network

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:44.290
to optimize hundreds of layers?

00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:45.750
I mean, come on, right?

00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:46.584
This is ridiculous.

00:20:46.584 --> 00:20:48.000
We would have to
go back and start

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:49.860
designing our filters again.

00:20:49.860 --> 00:20:53.300
Fast forward a few years,
and here we are, right?

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:58.740
Where, basically, we can now
launch a Jupiter notebook,

00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:01.890
and we basically have one of my
colleagues here doing a demo,

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:02.460
right?

00:21:02.460 --> 00:21:06.900
That basically trains on 1,000
images to classify flowers.

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:11.870
He gets the entire demo
done in like an hour.

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:14.880
You basically can classify a set
of images, get it all trained,

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:18.030
and you basically move on to
the next thing in your life.

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:20.340
That's essentially what
the cloud gives you, right?

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:22.770
The combination of
machine learning

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:26.400
and the computational
power of the cloud,

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:29.770
is what allows us to solve
that kind of problem.

00:21:29.770 --> 00:21:32.490
So where Kaz is a
developer advocate,

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:33.810
he's basically goes out.

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:35.610
And his role at Google
is to basically get

00:21:35.610 --> 00:21:38.357
everybody enthusiastic
about doing things.

00:21:38.357 --> 00:21:40.440
And basically explaining
how neural networks work,

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:41.640
and how he can do them.

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:44.160
I'm on the professional
services end.

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:46.080
And the people I deal
with are always asking,

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:48.870
so what kind of
problems can it solve?

00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:50.380
What can I do with it?

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:50.880
Right?

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:52.860
And my answer is, hint--

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:54.570
it's about the alternative.

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:57.300
It's about the things
that were alternative when

00:21:57.300 --> 00:21:58.770
I started in my career, right?

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:00.390
Instead of using
a neural network,

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:04.200
the alternative was that
I wrote heuristic rules,

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:05.970
image-processing filters.

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:09.450
But that same concept
carries through to a variety

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:11.960
of other situations.

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:13.710
Here, for example,
is Descartes Labs.

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:16.080
Descartes Labs is
one of GCP customers.

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:19.050
That wanted to basically
look at Landsat imagery.

00:22:19.050 --> 00:22:20.940
So now you have
Landsat imagery, they

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:22.620
want to look at the imagery.

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:25.740
And looking at the imagery, they
wanted to basically determine

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:30.060
how much corn or soybean
yield was going to happen

00:22:30.060 --> 00:22:31.260
across the entire country.

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:33.930
Because commodity's prices,
a variety of things,

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:37.070
depend on how much food
actually gets grown.

00:22:37.070 --> 00:22:39.990
Well, by looking at these
Landsat images, right?

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:42.180
Even before the
harvest happened.

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:44.550
They were able to
basically predict

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:48.550
the corn yield and
the soybean yield,

00:22:48.550 --> 00:22:53.500
and they were able to do it five
months before the actual USDA

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:56.430
report came out that said,
this is how much got done.

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:56.930
Right?

00:22:56.930 --> 00:22:58.980
You basically changed the input.

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:02.800
The input used to be-- you
harvested all of the stuff,

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:05.110
and you measured how
much went to market.

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:07.750
Instead, the input for
Descartes Labs was--

00:23:07.750 --> 00:23:08.980
let's just image the Earth.

00:23:08.980 --> 00:23:10.930
Let's use the
images of the Earth

00:23:10.930 --> 00:23:13.180
to basically figure out
where all the farms are

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:14.260
and how much--

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:16.660
Look at the greenery content.

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:18.880
Look at how these things change.

00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:20.360
And they could use
historical data.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:23.170
They had previous years
of data from a variety

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:25.600
of different locations,
and they could basically

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:27.659
use that to train their model.

00:23:27.659 --> 00:23:29.450
And they could basically
do the prediction.

00:23:29.450 --> 00:23:30.825
So the first thing
to think about

00:23:30.825 --> 00:23:33.020
is-- is there a
different way to do it?

00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:33.520
Right?

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:36.250
One way to do it would be
to basically wait, and wait

00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:37.570
for the analysis.

00:23:37.570 --> 00:23:39.610
Wait for the data
to get collected.

00:23:39.610 --> 00:23:43.180
The other way is to say, can
this information that I'm

00:23:43.180 --> 00:23:47.950
going to be doing, can I
get it from some other way?

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:50.230
And this some other
way for Descartes Labs

00:23:50.230 --> 00:23:52.900
was Landsat images.

00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:57.400
So these kinds of
use-cases are all

00:23:57.400 --> 00:23:59.300
across a variety of industries.

00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:01.150
So whether you're
in manufacturing,

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:04.450
you're in travel, you're in
retail, you're an energy,

00:24:04.450 --> 00:24:09.430
you're basically looking at what
data do I have And how can I

00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:14.260
use that data to basically go
ahead and predict or estimate

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:16.720
something that I
don't already know?

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:18.570
Or something that I will know--

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:21.460
like in Descartes Labs, they
would know the corn yield.

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:23.710
Except it would take them
five months to know it.

00:24:23.710 --> 00:24:26.340
And this is the way to basically
get to know it earlier.

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:28.690
By taking advantage of data.

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:31.450
And as Kaz pointed out,
the thing to realize

00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:37.660
is that, don't get
constrained by the way

00:24:37.660 --> 00:24:39.490
you're doing things now.

00:24:39.490 --> 00:24:41.931
The way a human would solve it--

00:24:41.931 --> 00:24:42.430
Right?

00:24:42.430 --> 00:24:46.120
Is not necessarily the way
the computer would solve it.

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:49.850
So you can easily imagine
feeding a computer

00:24:49.850 --> 00:24:52.750
a different type of
data set than the one

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:56.090
that you're currently doing
and using for analysis.

00:24:56.090 --> 00:25:00.160
So think about the data that is
somewhat predictive, somewhat

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:03.190
correlated with what's
actually the thing that you

00:25:03.190 --> 00:25:04.250
want to predict.

00:25:04.250 --> 00:25:05.110
OK?

00:25:05.110 --> 00:25:08.190
So remember I started out
by saying think about it.

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:09.370
It's about the alternative.

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:11.230
Eric Schmidt-- you
all heard him today.

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:12.280
An amazing speaker.

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:16.480
When I grow up, I want to speak
as well as Eric Schmidt speaks.

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:19.420
So Eric Schmidt, last
year at GCP Next--

00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:21.310
he basically talked
about machine learning.

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:24.270
He said machine learning-- this
is the next transformation.

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:26.530
It's the next transformation
for a lot of companies.

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:30.540
And the programming paradigm,
the way we program stuff,

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:32.260
is going to change.

00:25:32.260 --> 00:25:35.740
The way we program stuff
today is that we write rules.

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:36.850
We write if, then rules.

00:25:36.850 --> 00:25:38.680
We write loops, we
write iterations,

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:41.950
we write logic
into our programs.

00:25:41.950 --> 00:25:45.100
And Eric Schmidt is saying,
that's going to change.

00:25:45.100 --> 00:25:46.930
Instead of programming
a computer,

00:25:46.930 --> 00:25:49.940
you're going to show it
a bunch of data, right?

00:25:49.940 --> 00:25:53.960
You're going to basically
tell it to learn something.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:57.500
And the computers is basically
going to then do what you want.

00:25:57.500 --> 00:26:00.490
This is an interesting
quote, because he

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:04.940
has lots of fully-formed
thoughts here

00:26:04.940 --> 00:26:07.130
about what machine learning is.

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:10.070
But there is no word in
there about the thing

00:26:10.070 --> 00:26:12.500
that we normally think of
as machine learning, which

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:13.790
is learning from data.

00:26:13.790 --> 00:26:16.940
He's basically taking--
where do you start?

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:19.820
Think about the
heuristics and the rules

00:26:19.820 --> 00:26:22.460
that you currently
base your business on,

00:26:22.460 --> 00:26:25.470
and see if you can do those
things in a different way.

00:26:25.470 --> 00:26:28.250
So it's about the logic.

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:31.340
Figure out what you want
to do differently, and then

00:26:31.340 --> 00:26:34.380
collect the data you
need to do it that way.

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:34.880
Right?

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:36.680
So start, not with the data--

00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:38.960
start with the problem
you want to solve.

00:26:38.960 --> 00:26:41.210
The problem you
often want to solve

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:43.850
is a problem of scalability.

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:48.260
Because human heuristics,
hand-coded heuristics,

00:26:48.260 --> 00:26:50.610
don't really scale very well.

00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:54.860
So this is basically born
out of Google experience.

00:26:54.860 --> 00:26:56.910
The way Google
search used to work

00:26:56.910 --> 00:26:58.700
was you would go
to the search bar--

00:26:58.700 --> 00:27:01.670
and because someone
would say giants.

00:27:01.670 --> 00:27:04.460
And we need to show
you some results.

00:27:04.460 --> 00:27:07.730
And as we all know, the
top results on Google

00:27:07.730 --> 00:27:08.630
are very valuable.

00:27:08.630 --> 00:27:09.129
Right?

00:27:09.129 --> 00:27:14.820
So we want to be very thoughtful
about what is the top result.

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:19.050
We want to give our
searchers the right result.

00:27:19.050 --> 00:27:20.690
So the way we
would do this was--

00:27:20.690 --> 00:27:23.660
well somebody
searched for giants.

00:27:23.660 --> 00:27:25.790
Do we return them,
San Francisco Giants?

00:27:25.790 --> 00:27:28.810
Or do we return them,
New York Giants?

00:27:28.810 --> 00:27:30.400
Well there was a rule.

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:33.490
And the rule was if
the query are Giants,

00:27:33.490 --> 00:27:36.190
and if the user is
located in the Bay Area,

00:27:36.190 --> 00:27:39.070
show them results about
San Francisco Giants.

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:43.360
If the query is Giants, and the
user is located in New York,

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:45.820
show them results
about New York Giants.

00:27:45.820 --> 00:27:49.450
If the user is not in the Bay
Area, user is not in New York,

00:27:49.450 --> 00:27:52.400
show them results
about big, tall people.

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:53.130
Right?

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:58.630
That was basically the rule that
was in the Google search engine

00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:02.295
for one query about giants.

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:10.760
Think about how many queries
happen for every sports team,

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:12.470
for every river.

00:28:12.470 --> 00:28:14.630
Someone searches
for the Red River

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:17.330
there in Texas, we
show you the river

00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:19.490
between Oklahoma and Texas.

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:22.520
If the person is in Minnesota,
we show you the Minnesota Red

00:28:22.520 --> 00:28:23.720
River, right?

00:28:23.720 --> 00:28:28.010
So every geographic feature--
stadiums, names of businesses,

00:28:28.010 --> 00:28:28.510
right?

00:28:28.510 --> 00:28:31.500
And this is just about location.

00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:33.300
This is just about location.

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:36.840
You have so many other
cues about your interests.

00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:39.210
So you can easily imagine--

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:46.820
just imagine how big and complex
and hairy that code base gets.

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:52.800
That's not tractable.

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:56.940
And that's when
RankBrain came along.

00:28:56.940 --> 00:29:02.350
So RankBrain was let's
not hard code rules.

00:29:02.350 --> 00:29:05.450
Instead, let's see if we can
do things in a better way.

00:29:05.450 --> 00:29:07.360
We have data, right?

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:09.400
We know that when we
show people results,

00:29:09.400 --> 00:29:12.520
we know what they
actually clicked on.

00:29:12.520 --> 00:29:17.170
And let's use it to train a
model to basically predict

00:29:17.170 --> 00:29:19.750
using a whole host of inputs.

00:29:19.750 --> 00:29:22.870
Let's make a network to
go ahead and predict.

00:29:22.870 --> 00:29:24.910
What the top result ought to be.

00:29:24.910 --> 00:29:30.120
So we were replacing all
of those heuristic rules

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:32.700
by a neural network.

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:36.930
And that neural network,
as soon as it got released,

00:29:36.930 --> 00:29:40.440
it essentially became the
third most important signal

00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:43.320
among hundreds of
signals that decide

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:46.650
what the ranking of the page
is going to be on the Google

00:29:46.650 --> 00:29:49.240
search result.

00:29:49.240 --> 00:29:51.130
That's basically
what Eric Schmidt

00:29:51.130 --> 00:29:56.080
is driving at when he says that
machine learning is a new way

00:29:56.080 --> 00:29:57.940
of programming, right?

00:29:57.940 --> 00:30:00.790
Because what we're doing is
that we are replacing the way

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:02.870
we do logic.

00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:05.600
We are replacing
the way we do logic.

00:30:05.600 --> 00:30:10.190
And starting with that, knowing
you're successful in the thing

00:30:10.190 --> 00:30:14.420
that the company is most famous
for, everybody takes notice.

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:14.920
Right?

00:30:14.920 --> 00:30:17.405
So four years ago,
in 2012, there

00:30:17.405 --> 00:30:22.210
was pretty much no deep learning
modules in Google, right?

00:30:22.210 --> 00:30:27.160
As of quarter for 2016, we
are basically at 4,000 models.

00:30:27.160 --> 00:30:30.850
And this is all over
all of Google products.

00:30:30.850 --> 00:30:33.370
Apps, Gmail, Maps, Photos.

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:35.350
You guys have seen
it everywhere.

00:30:35.350 --> 00:30:40.510
And it's becomes this thing
that you basically get everybody

00:30:40.510 --> 00:30:43.180
in this mindset
of, we maybe doing

00:30:43.180 --> 00:30:45.790
things using a seat
of the pants rule now,

00:30:45.790 --> 00:30:47.290
but we're going to
collect the data.

00:30:47.290 --> 00:30:50.230
So we can do it with
machine learning later.

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:54.370
That's essentially going to be
the approach that every product

00:30:54.370 --> 00:30:55.540
team takes.

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:57.290
Every product team
essentially says,

00:30:57.290 --> 00:30:59.980
how can we solve
this problem with ML,

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:02.410
so we don't have to
keep writing rules.

00:31:02.410 --> 00:31:05.230
And handling
specific situations.

00:31:05.230 --> 00:31:09.130
And specific-- if this, if
that, all those situations.

00:31:09.130 --> 00:31:11.330
We want to avoid that
by doing ML, right?

00:31:11.330 --> 00:31:14.860
So you basically get
into this process of,

00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:16.150
how do I collect the data?

00:31:16.150 --> 00:31:18.970
So my point here is that
you start with the problem

00:31:18.970 --> 00:31:20.080
you want to solve.

00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:23.014
Think about the
scalability of your logic,

00:31:23.014 --> 00:31:24.430
and then think
about what data you

00:31:24.430 --> 00:31:25.690
need to collect to solve it.

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:33.350
First way-- if you are to infuse
your business with machine

00:31:33.350 --> 00:31:37.310
learning, the first thing to do
is to rethink your user input.

00:31:37.310 --> 00:31:38.060
Right?

00:31:38.060 --> 00:31:40.780
Rethink you user input.

00:31:40.780 --> 00:31:41.890
What do I mean by that?

00:31:41.890 --> 00:31:44.650
Well, you on one hand
have custom machine

00:31:44.650 --> 00:31:47.710
learning models, with
TensorFlow, Machine Learning

00:31:47.710 --> 00:31:50.290
Engine, with just
[INAUDIBLE] today, right?

00:31:50.290 --> 00:31:53.920
So you can basically build your
own machine learning models.

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:58.060
On the other hand, building
your own machine learning models

00:31:58.060 --> 00:32:02.697
requires lots and
lots and lots of data.

00:32:02.697 --> 00:32:04.780
So when we talk about--
you want to build an image

00:32:04.780 --> 00:32:09.700
classification model, you
need millions of images.

00:32:09.700 --> 00:32:12.190
But we have ways to,
basically, do things

00:32:12.190 --> 00:32:13.960
with fewer images, right?

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:17.440
We also have what are
pre-trained machine learning

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:18.530
models.

00:32:18.530 --> 00:32:21.760
The Vision API, which
is basically already

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:24.250
knows how to classify
lots of images.

00:32:24.250 --> 00:32:26.590
We have the Speech
API, which already

00:32:26.590 --> 00:32:29.050
knows how to transcribe text.

00:32:29.050 --> 00:32:31.540
It is a waste of your
time and resources

00:32:31.540 --> 00:32:35.190
to go build a speech model.

00:32:35.190 --> 00:32:39.720
You might just use ML
model, that we have trained

00:32:39.720 --> 00:32:42.220
over lots and lots of data.

00:32:42.220 --> 00:32:45.090
So when you look at our video
API that got demoed today,

00:32:45.090 --> 00:32:46.540
that's not on the slide yet.

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:47.040
Right?

00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:50.670
So that's basically
on-trained on so many more

00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:54.890
videos than it's even possible
for you to think about.

00:32:54.890 --> 00:32:55.860
Right?

00:32:55.860 --> 00:32:57.510
There are better
things for you to do.

00:32:57.510 --> 00:32:59.250
All of us that are
in businesses--

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:00.239
we have core focus.

00:33:00.239 --> 00:33:02.280
So those are the things
that we want to focus on.

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:05.710
The things that Google has done,
you want to take advantage of.

00:33:05.710 --> 00:33:07.740
And that's where the
pre-trained models come in.

00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:12.220
So number one, let
your users talk to you.

00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:16.340
The days when you could have
expected your users to come in

00:33:16.340 --> 00:33:19.580
and type are gone.

00:33:19.580 --> 00:33:20.710
OK.

00:33:20.710 --> 00:33:24.860
These days, if I
want to go somewhere,

00:33:24.860 --> 00:33:27.740
I'm basically on
Google Maps, right?

00:33:27.740 --> 00:33:31.310
I pretty much never, ever
type the name of the place

00:33:31.310 --> 00:33:32.090
I'm going to.

00:33:32.090 --> 00:33:34.430
It's all just voice
search, right?

00:33:34.430 --> 00:33:36.350
Especially because
I'm on the go.

00:33:36.350 --> 00:33:39.170
It is completely,
completely voice search.

00:33:39.170 --> 00:33:41.660
And maybe I'm on
the leading edge,

00:33:41.660 --> 00:33:48.470
but a huge fraction of searches
on mobile happen through voice.

00:33:48.470 --> 00:33:53.600
And when you build applications,
increasingly, your users

00:33:53.600 --> 00:33:56.490
are going to expect
the same thing.

00:33:56.490 --> 00:34:00.380
So for example, there's
a company called, AUCNET,

00:34:00.380 --> 00:34:04.220
which wanted to basically
build the system that

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:08.750
needed to figure out how
much is my car worth.

00:34:08.750 --> 00:34:12.480
So people have done
this before, right?

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:16.040
But what AUCNET said was, I'm
going to rethink my user input.

00:34:16.040 --> 00:34:17.550
And what I'm going to do is--

00:34:17.550 --> 00:34:19.633
you want to figure out how
much your car is worth,

00:34:19.633 --> 00:34:20.909
take a photograph of your car.

00:34:20.909 --> 00:34:23.492
Take a photograph of the front
of your car, of your dashboard,

00:34:23.492 --> 00:34:25.500
of the back, of the
sides, everything.

00:34:25.500 --> 00:34:27.330
Take a bunch of photographs.

00:34:27.330 --> 00:34:28.980
Send it to us.

00:34:28.980 --> 00:34:31.889
We'll tell you how
much the car is worth.

00:34:31.889 --> 00:34:35.730
so from the photographs,
AUCNET that would determine

00:34:35.730 --> 00:34:38.940
the model of the car, who
made the car, which year it

00:34:38.940 --> 00:34:42.270
was, the condition of the
car, all of that stuff.

00:34:42.270 --> 00:34:45.060
And tell you this
is what it's worth.

00:34:45.060 --> 00:34:48.270
Now put yourself in
somebody's situation, right?

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:49.350
You're a consumer.

00:34:49.350 --> 00:34:52.270
You have two possible websites.

00:34:52.270 --> 00:34:56.340
One website gives you a
form that's two pages long.

00:34:56.340 --> 00:35:00.240
And at their website says, send
me a couple of photographs.

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:05.080
You obviously see that now ML
is a competitive advantage.

00:35:05.080 --> 00:35:07.140
Rethink your user input.

00:35:07.140 --> 00:35:09.780
Your user input doesn't
need to be forms.

00:35:09.780 --> 00:35:10.280
Right?

00:35:10.280 --> 00:35:12.400
Your user input doesn't
need to be forms.

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:15.927
How many of you
use Google Forms?

00:35:15.927 --> 00:35:17.010
Quite a few of you, right?

00:35:17.010 --> 00:35:19.960
And have you noticed
that recently when

00:35:19.960 --> 00:35:22.440
you're creating a Google form--

00:35:22.440 --> 00:35:24.390
you can say, for example.

00:35:24.390 --> 00:35:27.120
You ask a question, when
do you want to meet?

00:35:27.120 --> 00:35:31.061
And it auto suggests answers.

00:35:31.061 --> 00:35:31.560
Right?

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:33.360
It auto suggests
possible answers

00:35:33.360 --> 00:35:35.711
that helps your
dynamically built stuff.

00:35:35.711 --> 00:35:36.210
Right?

00:35:36.210 --> 00:35:37.860
And that's essentially
the kind of stuff

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:38.901
that we're talking about.

00:35:38.901 --> 00:35:45.270
We're talking about being
able to know and limit

00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:48.030
the amount of user input that
somebody has to give you.

00:35:48.030 --> 00:35:51.840
Because you can guess
what their next question

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:54.330
or the next answer
is going to be.

00:35:54.330 --> 00:35:57.770
Here's another example of
using images to take action.

00:35:57.770 --> 00:36:00.210
The application is
called Meeting Nanny.

00:36:00.210 --> 00:36:00.780
OK.

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:05.210
Which basically has a
camera on a conference room.

00:36:05.210 --> 00:36:08.150
The same camera that's used
for video conferencing.

00:36:08.150 --> 00:36:10.730
Again, it's connected
to the calendar.

00:36:10.730 --> 00:36:13.390
So all it does is basically
looks at the room.

00:36:13.390 --> 00:36:16.600
So the meeting was supposed
to start seven minutes later.

00:36:16.600 --> 00:36:18.220
Nobody has shown up.

00:36:18.220 --> 00:36:22.740
It will release the room so
somebody else can come use it.

00:36:22.740 --> 00:36:27.080
So using images to take action.

00:36:27.080 --> 00:36:30.540
The user input here is
all completely automated.

00:36:30.540 --> 00:36:31.910
But it's an image.

00:36:31.910 --> 00:36:32.600
It's an image.

00:36:32.600 --> 00:36:34.610
It's an image of the
room, so it can figure out

00:36:34.610 --> 00:36:37.120
if you can do this or not.

00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:39.630
Another customer for us, Ocado.

00:36:39.630 --> 00:36:42.260
The way Ocado used to work
was-- somebody sent an email.

00:36:42.260 --> 00:36:44.390
You had somebody sit
there, read the email.

00:36:44.390 --> 00:36:47.690
Figure out what was in the
email and decide what to do.

00:36:47.690 --> 00:36:50.990
The way they do it now is that
they use the natural language

00:36:50.990 --> 00:36:52.520
processing, API.

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:56.026
Again, a pre-built model,
the NLP, API model.

00:36:56.026 --> 00:36:57.150
They were able to use that.

00:36:57.150 --> 00:37:00.830
And they can use that model
to basically say, oh, here's

00:37:00.830 --> 00:37:03.380
an email that came
in, and this email

00:37:03.380 --> 00:37:06.600
is a customer
expressing satisfaction.

00:37:06.600 --> 00:37:08.090
So this is what I need to do.

00:37:08.090 --> 00:37:10.520
This is something
that's about billing.

00:37:10.520 --> 00:37:12.800
The customers is
frustrated about building,

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:16.580
so I need to send it to
somebody who handles billing.

00:37:16.580 --> 00:37:18.830
So an email comes
into the company,

00:37:18.830 --> 00:37:24.650
it automatically gets triaged,
sent to the right department.

00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:27.320
This is the kind of stuff
that your customers are

00:37:27.320 --> 00:37:29.270
going to increasingly expect.

00:37:29.270 --> 00:37:29.870
Right?

00:37:29.870 --> 00:37:31.940
You're not going to
be able to get away

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:36.830
with form responses that
are just boilerplate stuff.

00:37:36.830 --> 00:37:40.520
It needs to be really, really,
really meaningful responses.

00:37:40.520 --> 00:37:41.750
And you can do this.

00:37:41.750 --> 00:37:45.910
You can do this now
with machine learning.

00:37:45.910 --> 00:37:49.660
Another example
here, lots of us,

00:37:49.660 --> 00:37:51.110
when we build applications--

00:37:51.110 --> 00:37:53.590
We ask for customer images.

00:37:53.590 --> 00:37:55.150
That's a scary thing.

00:37:55.150 --> 00:37:58.540
Because we are all working in a
variety of different industries

00:37:58.540 --> 00:38:01.150
that have regulatory stuff.

00:38:01.150 --> 00:38:04.540
So here's a customer that
wanted to basically prevent

00:38:04.540 --> 00:38:07.491
violent images from
being uploaded.

00:38:07.491 --> 00:38:07.990
OK.

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:13.690
And they also wanted to
prevent unsafe for work images.

00:38:13.690 --> 00:38:16.390
And here's an example of
the Vision API at work.

00:38:16.390 --> 00:38:18.550
It's able to focus in on
the image on the left,

00:38:18.550 --> 00:38:21.670
and say, OK, this is
actually a safe image.

00:38:21.670 --> 00:38:23.620
It's just showing a
couple on a beach.

00:38:23.620 --> 00:38:26.200
But the image on the
right, it basically

00:38:26.200 --> 00:38:30.160
flags as being unsafe.

00:38:30.160 --> 00:38:32.050
And it turns out,
that if you look

00:38:32.050 --> 00:38:35.160
at what the machine learning
algorithm is focusing on--

00:38:35.160 --> 00:38:37.990
that's the yellow stuff
and the red stuff.

00:38:37.990 --> 00:38:42.430
It's focusing on the finger
of the person holding the gun,

00:38:42.430 --> 00:38:45.650
and apparently that person
is squeezing the trigger.

00:38:45.650 --> 00:38:48.130
So this is showing
somebody actually

00:38:48.130 --> 00:38:50.020
in the process of shooting.

00:38:50.020 --> 00:38:52.750
Knows that this is
now a violent thing

00:38:52.750 --> 00:38:54.390
and removing that violent scene.

00:38:54.390 --> 00:38:59.110
It's at that level of crispness.

00:38:59.110 --> 00:39:00.810
Is what ML operate at.

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:07.220
Another thing that you can do.

00:39:07.220 --> 00:39:09.680
So he can re-imagine
your user inputs.

00:39:09.680 --> 00:39:11.040
That's one thing, right?

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:13.370
You basically look at images.

00:39:13.370 --> 00:39:16.950
Treat images as bonafide inputs.

00:39:16.950 --> 00:39:19.730
Think of speech
as bonafied input.

00:39:19.730 --> 00:39:22.946
Your input doesn't just
need to be text, right?

00:39:22.946 --> 00:39:24.320
That's what we
normally think of.

00:39:24.320 --> 00:39:25.694
We're writing
computer programs--

00:39:25.694 --> 00:39:26.930
If they are input, it's text.

00:39:26.930 --> 00:39:28.388
It doesn't need to
be text anymore.

00:39:28.388 --> 00:39:29.270
It can be images.

00:39:29.270 --> 00:39:30.704
It can be audio.

00:39:30.704 --> 00:39:32.870
But the other thing that
you may want to think about

00:39:32.870 --> 00:39:35.190
is your business
processes themselves.

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:38.090
Re-imagine the way you
do your business process.

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:40.010
Change your business process.

00:39:40.010 --> 00:39:41.370
What do you mean by that, right?

00:39:41.370 --> 00:39:44.510
So if you're thinking about
ML as a rocket engine.

00:39:44.510 --> 00:39:46.280
The thing that can
take you places.

00:39:46.280 --> 00:39:48.500
The fuel is going to be data.

00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:51.106
As long as you have data,
you can go places with it.

00:39:51.106 --> 00:39:53.480
So what are the kinds of things
that we're talking about,

00:39:53.480 --> 00:39:53.980
right?

00:39:53.980 --> 00:39:58.280
Your typical customer journey
here in business processes--

00:39:58.280 --> 00:40:00.920
your typical customer
journey is to go look

00:40:00.920 --> 00:40:05.840
at any kind of manual
thing that you are doing.

00:40:05.840 --> 00:40:09.810
And seeing if you can do that
manual think in a better way.

00:40:09.810 --> 00:40:13.100
So this is global fishing
watch, another GCP customer.

00:40:13.100 --> 00:40:16.520
And they basically
would do manual analysis

00:40:16.520 --> 00:40:18.230
of global fishing fleets.

00:40:18.230 --> 00:40:22.700
So the idea was that they would
look at where the ships are.

00:40:22.700 --> 00:40:25.100
Basically figure out
how often the ships had

00:40:25.100 --> 00:40:27.320
gone to a particular
region to, basically,

00:40:27.320 --> 00:40:29.870
see if that region
was being overfished.

00:40:29.870 --> 00:40:32.510
Whether somebody was
fishing above their quota.

00:40:32.510 --> 00:40:36.980
Whether somebody was using the
fishery resources more often

00:40:36.980 --> 00:40:37.820
than they should.

00:40:37.820 --> 00:40:40.340
Or that they are going
into protected waters.

00:40:40.340 --> 00:40:42.112
All of that kind of stuff.

00:40:42.112 --> 00:40:43.070
And they would do this.

00:40:43.070 --> 00:40:47.660
They had manual analyst
doing this all the time.

00:40:47.660 --> 00:40:50.137
Some of that stuff
could be automated.

00:40:50.137 --> 00:40:51.470
And that's what they did, right?

00:40:51.470 --> 00:40:56.115
So with machine learning,
you could basically--

00:40:56.115 --> 00:40:59.030
think about a lot of things
that were done manually.

00:40:59.030 --> 00:41:01.885
They didn't automate
the whole thing,

00:41:01.885 --> 00:41:03.260
but they could
basically automate

00:41:03.260 --> 00:41:05.020
different stages of it.

00:41:05.020 --> 00:41:08.060
To, basically, then
allow the analysts

00:41:08.060 --> 00:41:10.640
to basically bring their
experience and expertise

00:41:10.640 --> 00:41:12.680
to the parts that
really mattered.

00:41:12.680 --> 00:41:16.340
And basically make the routine
stuff so much more easier,

00:41:16.340 --> 00:41:17.761
so that it could
be done with ML.

00:41:17.761 --> 00:41:18.260
OK.

00:41:18.260 --> 00:41:21.290
So the typical customer
journey often here

00:41:21.290 --> 00:41:24.800
involves find the things
that your analysts are doing.

00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:26.660
The things that they hate doing.

00:41:26.660 --> 00:41:28.324
Because they come
in the morning,

00:41:28.324 --> 00:41:30.740
and they're just doing the
same thing over and over again.

00:41:30.740 --> 00:41:33.260
They get tired, because
it's very repetitive.

00:41:33.260 --> 00:41:35.810
And there's the kinds of
things that you can automate.

00:41:35.810 --> 00:41:38.630
Because, A, you have
the results of all

00:41:38.630 --> 00:41:41.600
of that manual analysis
that stretches back

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:43.560
many, many, many years.

00:41:43.560 --> 00:41:45.240
That's the key thing, right?

00:41:45.240 --> 00:41:47.210
What you're thinking
about is what kind of data

00:41:47.210 --> 00:41:50.050
do you have so you can
actually automate it.

00:41:50.050 --> 00:41:51.440
So the data that
you will have is

00:41:51.440 --> 00:41:54.560
because you've done this
analysis in a manual way

00:41:54.560 --> 00:41:55.970
for so long.

00:41:55.970 --> 00:42:00.080
You, basically, may have five
years, 10 years, 15 years--

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:02.490
how many years your
business has been around.

00:42:02.490 --> 00:42:04.890
Or how many years your
industry has been around.

00:42:04.890 --> 00:42:08.340
Of the results of
that manual analysis.

00:42:08.340 --> 00:42:10.440
And you can basically take
all of the inputs that

00:42:10.440 --> 00:42:14.160
go into manual analysis,
and you take the outcome,

00:42:14.160 --> 00:42:17.000
and it can train
an ML model on it.

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:20.420
Because all you need is
the input and the outcome.

00:42:20.420 --> 00:42:22.820
Don't worry about,
oh, in order to do

00:42:22.820 --> 00:42:25.730
this person looked at this other
thing, and all of that stuff.

00:42:25.730 --> 00:42:26.660
That's fine.

00:42:26.660 --> 00:42:28.610
That may be the way
the human did it,

00:42:28.610 --> 00:42:31.370
but the ML model may be
able to basically hone in

00:42:31.370 --> 00:42:35.270
on other things that
are representative

00:42:35.270 --> 00:42:40.530
of the situations in concerned.

00:42:40.530 --> 00:42:43.260
But when you're
talking about data,

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:47.010
most of our data analysis,
as humans, most of our data

00:42:47.010 --> 00:42:49.010
analysis often--

00:42:49.010 --> 00:42:52.050
if you go to look at our data
scientists or data engineers.

00:42:52.050 --> 00:42:54.090
Our data analysts
in the company.

00:42:54.090 --> 00:42:57.160
What kind of data are
they dealing with?

00:42:57.160 --> 00:42:58.510
Relational databases.

00:42:58.510 --> 00:43:00.550
They're dealing with
structured data.

00:43:00.550 --> 00:43:04.420
But in a typical
company, 90% of your data

00:43:04.420 --> 00:43:06.330
will be unstructured data.

00:43:06.330 --> 00:43:08.420
What do you mean by
unstructured data?

00:43:08.420 --> 00:43:08.920
Emails.

00:43:14.340 --> 00:43:17.570
Tons of information
in your e-mails.

00:43:17.570 --> 00:43:18.760
Reports.

00:43:18.760 --> 00:43:21.070
Lots and lots of
information when

00:43:21.070 --> 00:43:23.170
somebody goes to a
field site and writes

00:43:23.170 --> 00:43:25.840
a whole bunch of field notes.

00:43:25.840 --> 00:43:29.100
What are you doing with
that data right now?

00:43:29.100 --> 00:43:31.740
It's your company's data, but
you can't do anything with it.

00:43:31.740 --> 00:43:35.310
Because, A, you're thinking
I cannot actually put it

00:43:35.310 --> 00:43:36.970
in the structured form.

00:43:36.970 --> 00:43:41.100
So I can write a
SQL query on it.

00:43:41.100 --> 00:43:43.710
But that's where ML can help.

00:43:43.710 --> 00:43:49.900
ML can help you find insight
from unstructured data.

00:43:49.900 --> 00:43:53.520
But unstructured data is hard.

00:43:53.520 --> 00:43:55.360
Unstructured data is hard.

00:43:55.360 --> 00:43:57.650
OK, I did an ML
BootCamp yesterday.

00:43:57.650 --> 00:44:00.240
And we basically talked about
how to do structured data.

00:44:00.240 --> 00:44:01.685
Because that's easy.

00:44:01.685 --> 00:44:03.060
When you're doing
a BootCamp, you

00:44:03.060 --> 00:44:07.170
want to show people
how to do things--

00:44:07.170 --> 00:44:07.990
the easy thing.

00:44:07.990 --> 00:44:08.490
Right?

00:44:08.490 --> 00:44:10.710
Let's get started with ML.

00:44:10.710 --> 00:44:12.640
We'll get started
with structured data.

00:44:12.640 --> 00:44:16.320
Unstructured data,
images, speak, text.

00:44:16.320 --> 00:44:17.750
Those are hard.

00:44:17.750 --> 00:44:18.630
Those are very hard.

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:21.300
That's where you
basically go higher PhDs.

00:44:21.300 --> 00:44:23.030
To come do the
work for you there.

00:44:23.030 --> 00:44:23.530
Right?

00:44:23.530 --> 00:44:26.900
But you don't need to
do that all the time.

00:44:26.900 --> 00:44:29.030
Because what you can
do is that you can

00:44:29.030 --> 00:44:30.800
take your unstructured data.

00:44:30.800 --> 00:44:33.680
Whether it's images, whether
it's audio, whether it's video,

00:44:33.680 --> 00:44:36.590
whether it's free
form text, and you

00:44:36.590 --> 00:44:41.120
can use the pre-trained
of models out of GCP.

00:44:41.120 --> 00:44:43.070
The Vision API for images.

00:44:43.070 --> 00:44:44.320
What is it going to do?

00:44:44.320 --> 00:44:46.850
It's going to look at
your image and tell you

00:44:46.850 --> 00:44:48.740
what's in the image.

00:44:48.740 --> 00:44:52.100
It's going to tell
you this image

00:44:52.100 --> 00:44:54.770
has a mountain pass in it.

00:44:54.770 --> 00:44:57.590
This image has a [INAUDIBLE]
in it, et cetera.

00:44:57.590 --> 00:44:59.090
It's going to tell
you what's in it.

00:44:59.090 --> 00:45:03.980
And you can also build on
top of our image models

00:45:03.980 --> 00:45:07.460
to basically classify your
own particular things.

00:45:07.460 --> 00:45:11.240
You want to basically determine
that this image contains

00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:14.030
part number 8341.

00:45:14.030 --> 00:45:14.870
No problem, right?

00:45:14.870 --> 00:45:19.010
Give us enough images
of parts 8341 and 8348.

00:45:19.010 --> 00:45:21.290
And we can basically tell
you which one's which.

00:45:21.290 --> 00:45:23.165
Which one is a four inch
screw, and which one

00:45:23.165 --> 00:45:24.080
is a six inch screw.

00:45:24.080 --> 00:45:29.750
Those are pretty easy if you
build on top of our models.

00:45:29.750 --> 00:45:31.630
So you can do images that way.

00:45:31.630 --> 00:45:32.720
You have audio.

00:45:32.720 --> 00:45:34.490
Use a Speech API.

00:45:34.490 --> 00:45:37.255
And you basically take
that and it'll you text.

00:45:37.255 --> 00:45:38.630
So what do you do
with that text?

00:45:38.630 --> 00:45:40.300
It's free form text.

00:45:40.300 --> 00:45:44.170
And if you have free form text,
use a natural language API.

00:45:44.170 --> 00:45:46.340
So what the natural
language API will do

00:45:46.340 --> 00:45:49.550
is-- a given free form
text, it will tell you

00:45:49.550 --> 00:45:53.160
who's mentioned, what's
mentioned, the sentiment.

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:56.240
It'll tell you this is a full
sentence, this is a phrase,

00:45:56.240 --> 00:45:58.540
these are the links between
these things, and so on.

00:45:58.540 --> 00:45:59.040
Right?

00:45:59.040 --> 00:46:00.410
So you can basically do that.

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:02.120
You can do video.

00:46:02.120 --> 00:46:03.050
We just saw that.

00:46:03.050 --> 00:46:06.660
So all of those APIs
essentially give you

00:46:06.660 --> 00:46:13.580
places, labels, people, events,
sentiment, and at that point

00:46:13.580 --> 00:46:17.180
that is a perfect thing
to go ahead and feed

00:46:17.180 --> 00:46:20.990
into your custom-machine
learning model.

00:46:20.990 --> 00:46:25.460
And now, it's not as
hard a problem anymore.

00:46:25.460 --> 00:46:29.374
Because you're now dealing with
essentially-structured data.

00:46:29.374 --> 00:46:31.290
And you're basically
deriving insight from it.

00:46:31.290 --> 00:46:33.170
We've done the
hard part of going

00:46:33.170 --> 00:46:37.130
from unstructured data to
things that you can now

00:46:37.130 --> 00:46:39.620
do your model training on.

00:46:43.060 --> 00:46:45.070
The third way that
you can basically

00:46:45.070 --> 00:46:49.540
use ML in your business
is to delight your users.

00:46:49.540 --> 00:46:51.970
And what I mean by
delight your users

00:46:51.970 --> 00:46:56.680
is to basically figure out what
your users want before even

00:46:56.680 --> 00:46:59.430
they realize they want.

00:46:59.430 --> 00:47:04.420
To basically do that,
that's part of delight.

00:47:04.420 --> 00:47:06.880
For example, how
many of you thought

00:47:06.880 --> 00:47:09.670
that your email program
had to suggest to you what

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:13.120
the response was going to be?

00:47:13.120 --> 00:47:18.700
But now, 20% of all
users of inbox--

00:47:18.700 --> 00:47:20.590
and now Gmail also has it.

00:47:20.590 --> 00:47:21.760
Right?

00:47:21.760 --> 00:47:25.540
20% of all responses
on mobile to an email

00:47:25.540 --> 00:47:29.620
start out with the Smart reply.

00:47:29.620 --> 00:47:33.060
How many of you have
used Smart reply.

00:47:33.060 --> 00:47:34.380
Still getting out there, right?

00:47:34.380 --> 00:47:34.950
20%.

00:47:34.950 --> 00:47:36.360
It's still 20%.

00:47:36.360 --> 00:47:39.720
Basically, the way it
works is you get an email,

00:47:39.720 --> 00:47:43.620
and the email program,
inbox or Gmail,

00:47:43.620 --> 00:47:46.290
suggest to you with
three possible responses

00:47:46.290 --> 00:47:47.780
to that email.

00:47:47.780 --> 00:47:50.220
So for example, in
this particular email,

00:47:50.220 --> 00:47:52.950
this guy says, can
you send me something?

00:47:52.950 --> 00:47:56.220
And basically, the
three responses

00:47:56.220 --> 00:47:58.750
are-- one of them is
I'm working on them.

00:47:58.750 --> 00:48:00.870
The second one is I
just sent them to you.

00:48:00.870 --> 00:48:03.900
And the third one
is no plans yet.

00:48:03.900 --> 00:48:07.360
Three pretty good responses
that cover the spectrum.

00:48:07.360 --> 00:48:08.490
So you can start with that.

00:48:08.490 --> 00:48:10.755
You basically saved the
person three words of typing.

00:48:17.470 --> 00:48:20.710
So this can apply to your
business as well, right?

00:48:20.710 --> 00:48:26.680
If somebody comes, and they ring
the bell in your grocery shop,

00:48:26.680 --> 00:48:31.120
and you already know that the
location that they're in--

00:48:31.120 --> 00:48:33.970
the shelf is empty.

00:48:33.970 --> 00:48:37.870
You can actually delight
them by saying, we realize

00:48:37.870 --> 00:48:41.350
that we are out of
product x, would you

00:48:41.350 --> 00:48:43.780
like us to give you, right?

00:48:43.780 --> 00:48:46.300
These are the
three alternatives.

00:48:46.300 --> 00:48:49.000
You can take a rain check,
we can offer you a discount

00:48:49.000 --> 00:48:51.130
on another product, et cetera.

00:48:51.130 --> 00:48:53.090
Imagine how the
user's experience

00:48:53.090 --> 00:48:55.010
is going to be different from--

00:48:55.010 --> 00:48:58.650
they call in, and we
basically have them wait.

00:48:58.650 --> 00:49:01.840
Instead of running them through
the rigmarole, the moment

00:49:01.840 --> 00:49:03.430
they basically say, I need help.

00:49:03.430 --> 00:49:07.000
At this point, you basically
realize what help they need.

00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:11.400
Because you know what they
are currently experiencing.

00:49:11.400 --> 00:49:14.660
We are probably seeing some
of that already, right?

00:49:14.660 --> 00:49:17.420
These days when my
flight gets delayed,

00:49:17.420 --> 00:49:20.210
I automatically get rebooked
and the airline companies

00:49:20.210 --> 00:49:21.410
are already doing that.

00:49:21.410 --> 00:49:23.785
That's the kind of stuff that
we're talking about, right?

00:49:23.785 --> 00:49:25.850
You know what your
customers are experiencing.

00:49:25.850 --> 00:49:30.080
Basically, solve right there.

00:49:30.080 --> 00:49:33.300
Many things don't have to go
through the normal business

00:49:33.300 --> 00:49:33.800
process.

00:49:33.800 --> 00:49:38.230
If you know what they
are, you can predict it.

00:49:42.380 --> 00:49:45.680
Now in terms of solving a
problem that you didn't know

00:49:45.680 --> 00:49:46.730
existed, right?

00:49:46.730 --> 00:49:49.790
In a completely
different fancy way.

00:49:49.790 --> 00:49:53.660
Lots of people create their
own home videos, right?

00:49:53.660 --> 00:49:57.090
And they want to put a
background music track on it.

00:49:57.090 --> 00:50:00.170
When you're putting a background
music track on that video,

00:50:00.170 --> 00:50:01.970
what do what do people do?

00:50:01.970 --> 00:50:04.630
They basically get
a popular track,

00:50:04.630 --> 00:50:06.150
and they throw it
onto the video.

00:50:06.150 --> 00:50:08.330
And then YouTube
basically shuts it down,

00:50:08.330 --> 00:50:10.280
because of a
copyright violation.

00:50:10.280 --> 00:50:14.450
Because you're using music
that you shouldn't be using.

00:50:14.450 --> 00:50:18.710
But ML is now good enough
that you can basically

00:50:18.710 --> 00:50:19.724
go to a video.

00:50:19.724 --> 00:50:21.140
And you can basically
say, I would

00:50:21.140 --> 00:50:26.000
like you to generate
music for me that's jazz.

00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:28.990
That is low key for the
first three minutes,

00:50:28.990 --> 00:50:35.390
it crescendos between minutes
3 and 3.30, comes back down,

00:50:35.390 --> 00:50:38.850
and then basically
ends with a flourish.

00:50:38.850 --> 00:50:41.090
And that music
can get generated.

00:50:41.090 --> 00:50:42.540
It'll be jazz.

00:50:42.540 --> 00:50:47.090
It won't be Wynton
Marsalis for any means.

00:50:47.090 --> 00:50:50.329
But it'll be pretty darn
good for a home video.

00:50:50.329 --> 00:50:52.620
Those are the kinds of things
that we're talking about,

00:50:52.620 --> 00:50:53.920
right?

00:50:53.920 --> 00:50:56.390
I'm so excited about
what ML can do.

00:50:56.390 --> 00:50:58.800
I'm so excited about what
you guys can do with ML.

00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:00.520
So thank you all very much.

00:51:00.520 --> 00:51:04.720
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:07.120 --> 00:51:11.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

